    <div class=3D"at-a-glance">
      <div class=3D"glance-title">At a Glance</div>
      <div class=3D"glance-sections">
        <div class=3D"glance-section"><strong>What's working:</strong> You =
run Claude like an engineering org, not a chatbot =E2=80=94 front-loading B=
MAD-style briefs, PRDs and architecture before any code, then fanning work =
out across five to eight parallel lanes and reconverging with verified synt=
hesis. Just as important, you don't accept 'it compiles' as done: you deman=
d live browser previews, screenshot verification, and green suites before a=
nything lands. That combination took a Notion workflow to a full monorepo a=
nd drove several native macOS apps to shipping state, and you still push ba=
ck at review gates rather than rubber-stamping scope. <a href=3D"file:///Us=
ers/jawrsh/.claude/usage-data/report-2026-08-11-150422.html#section-wins" c=
lass=3D"see-more">Impressive Things You Did =E2=86=92</a></div>
        <div class=3D"glance-section"><strong>What's hindering you:</strong=
> On Claude's side, the recurring pattern is work that passes green gates b=
ut breaks the moment you touch it by hand =E2=80=94 silent sync failures, a=
 crash on a common action, a decode bug quietly dropping data. There's also=
 a tax from the scaffolding itself: check scripts with false positives, reg=
ex bugs, and parallel lanes colliding on shared type definitions. On your s=
ide, the friction is scope handoff =E2=80=94 'let's proceed' on a seven-pha=
se rewrite means aesthetic and architectural decisions get made without you=
, and you only see the result once it's expensive to reject. A few /doctor =
and diagnostic runs also died to interruptions because slow probes blocked =
your terminal. <a href=3D"file:///Users/jawrsh/.claude/usage-data/report-20=
26-08-11-150422.html#section-friction" class=3D"see-more">Where Things Go W=
rong =E2=86=92</a></div>
        <div class=3D"glance-section"><strong>Quick wins to try:</strong> A=
dd a Round 0 'foundation' step to every swarm that lands shared types and m=
odule boundaries before any lane spawns =E2=80=94 that alone kills your dup=
licate-type merge breakages. Turn your verification patterns into Custom Sk=
ills so a check script has to prove it can fail against a known-bad state b=
efore its PASS is trusted, and use Hooks to auto-run a runtime smoke pass (=
boot the app, exercise the top flow) at the end of each round. For redesign=
 work, ask for two or three cheap visual options on one screen before commi=
tting to a direction. <a href=3D"file:///Users/jawrsh/.claude/usage-data/re=
port-2026-08-11-150422.html#section-features" class=3D"see-more">Features t=
o Try =E2=86=92</a></div>
        <div class=3D"glance-section"><strong>Ambitious workflows:</strong>=
 Make adversarial hardening a default gate, not a one-off: a red-team agent=
 whose only job is to break what was just built =E2=80=94 fuzzing real data=
 shapes, forcing error paths, clicking destructive actions =E2=80=94 paired=
 with a fix agent that iterates until the red team comes up empty. You alre=
ady proved this works when it surfaced thirty bugs in your mail client; ins=
titutionalizing it means you stop being the QA department. Beyond that, aim=
 for contract-first orchestration where shared types and file-ownership bou=
ndaries are frozen in a machine-readable manifest before workers spawn, whi=
ch is what lets you scale past a dozen lanes and eventually run approved ep=
ics unattended overnight =E2=80=94 build, verify, red-team, self-repair =E2=
=80=94 with a morning briefing of screenshots, test deltas, and only the qu=
estions that genuinely need you. <a href=3D"file:///Users/jawrsh/.claude/us=
