    <h2 id=3D"section-usage">How You Use Claude Code</h2>
    <div class=3D"narrative">
      <p>You operate as an orchestrator, not a typist. Across 60 sessions a=
nd 333 commits you rarely hand Claude a single file to edit =E2=80=94 you h=
and it an epic. Sessions like 'build a Raindrop.io clone from an empty dire=
ctory', 'port this Photoshop dithering plugin to a web app', or 'build a na=
tive macOS site-capture tool via parallel workers' show your default move i=
s to <strong>delegate whole product surfaces and let multi-agent swarms run=
 for hours</strong>. Your tool profile confirms it: 4,873 Bash calls dwarf =
everything else, and the presence of TaskUpdate, Agent, and Ringer/BMAD orc=
hestration skills means you've built scaffolding so Claude can run unsuperv=
ised rather than checking in constantly. Terse go-signals like "let's proce=
ed" on outstanding Fume work are enough for you =E2=80=94 you expect Claude=
 to know the backlog and close it.</p>
<p>But you are not a hands-off delegator. You are a <strong>gate-keeper who=
 trusts the process and verifies the artifact</strong>. You repeatedly inse=
rt review gates (the MTHFL brief/PRD cycle, where you pushed back on upload=
-first/no-accounts v1 scoping as an adoption dealbreaker), you do gate walk=
s on shipped builds, and you find the bugs Claude's test suite missed by ha=
nd =E2=80=94 the silent sync failure and the 'Triage everything' render cra=
sh in the bookmark manager, the 500 iPhone tabs silently dropped by a decod=
e bug. Your 'buggy_code' friction count of 23 isn't Claude being sloppy so =
much as <strong>you actually exercising the product rather than trusting gr=
een checkmarks</strong>. When output misses, you say so bluntly and immedia=
tely: the R22 notch render 'missed the visual target', the collapsed state =
'looked strange', and after a seven-phase portfolio redesign you flatly sai=
d you weren't 'crazy about any of this' and stopped to reassess alone. You =
give reference material =E2=80=94 Kaleidoscope screenshots, Dynamic Island =
examples, client FigJam feedback, Figma annotations =E2=80=94 and you expec=
t fidelity to it.</p>
<p>Your interruptions are surgical rather than nervous. Twice you killed /d=
octor mid-diagnostic, and you complained hard when Claude's probe runs stol=
e window focus during a live screen-shared Zoom call =E2=80=94 forcing a re=
design to read Chrome's visibilityState instead. That's the pattern: <stron=
g>you interrupt when Claude's process intrudes on your environment, not whe=
n its work is slow</strong>. You're also unusually invested in the meta-lay=
er, spending whole sessions vetting ~20 candidate AI tools (flagging one as=
 fraud), auditioning Kimi 3 against K2.7-code on cost/quality, and building=
 layered CLAUDE.md configs across global/facet/project tiers. The satisfact=
ion data reflects it =E2=80=94 116 satisfied-or-better signals against 7 di=
ssatisfied =E2=80=94 and your closers tend to be short and decisive: "Looks=
 great! Let's ship it!"</p>
      <div class=3D"key-insight"><strong>Key pattern:</strong> You hand off=
 entire epics to autonomous agent swarms, then act as a hard-nosed QA gate =
=E2=80=94 hand-testing the result, citing reference material, and bluntly r=
ejecting anything that misses.</div>
    </div>
